Title: "Axiom Systems and Size"
Id: "4183ca80-b7a4-47be-9619-0f46dafc8553"


Text: "The elementary constructors do not by themselves constitute the usual
       Zermelo--Fraenkel theory. Modern set theory also controls how formulas
       select members of an existing set, how functional assignments produce new
       sets, and how membership chains behave. These principles are stated as
       schemes because one axiom is supplied for every suitable formula in the
       language."
Id: "ddb95a61-dddb-4c5a-bb15-430be7091d5a"


SectionTitle: "Separation and Replacement"
Id: "b04301f4-2e38-4797-b6cd-626d4be8bcb7"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_) is (_ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then:
    . have: x "in" B
      iff:
      . x "in"? A
      . phi(x)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Separation"
. description: "For every property $\varphi$, the elements of an existing set
                $A$ satisfying $\varphi$ form a set."
Id: "5077fbc7-a5c8-4d60-b1a7-a9d65097f003"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_, y_) is (_ is \set, _ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y is \set
    suchThat: phi(x, y)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: y is \set
    then:
    . have: y "in" B
      iff:
      . exists: x "in" A
        suchThat: phi(x, y)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Replacement"
. description: "The image of a set under a formula that assigns each input a
                unique output is again a set."
Id: "6e09ea04-f1dd-41c0-a0f5-4c962cd819ac"


Text: "Separation selects members already lying in one set; replacement sends
       members of a set to uniquely determined outputs. The distinction becomes
       important in transfinite constructions. Replacement ensures that the
       values obtained at all earlier stages still form a set to which union or
       another constructor can be applied."
Id: "1cbdad45-8bc2-4388-8a12-19b187499517"


SectionTitle: "Foundation and Choice"
Id: "960e9966-e880-421a-afa0-c8c753476a7c"


Axiom:
given: A is \(nonempty)::set
then:
. exists: a is \set
  suchThat:
  . a "in"? A
  . not:
    . exists: x is \set
      suchThat:
      . x "in"? A
      . x "in"? a
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Foundation"
. description: "Every nonempty set $A$ has a member disjoint from $A$."
Id: "cc3fc1c3-8ea9-45a6-874c-9215525952f4"


Axiom:
given:
. I, U is \set
. A(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
where:
. forAll: i "in" I
  then:
  . exists: S is \set
    suchThat:
    . S = A(i)
    . S != \empty.set
then:
. exists: f(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{U}
  suchThat:
  . forAll: i "in" I
    then:
    . exists: S is \set
      suchThat:
      . S = A(i)
      . f(i) "in"? S
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Choice"
. description: "Every set-indexed family of nonempty subsets of an ambient set
                admits a choice function selecting one element from each member
                of the family. This ambient-set formulation is equivalent over
                ZF to the usual formulation for arbitrary set-indexed families."
Id: "3f1ec40e-207c-431e-918e-7a8f4f23fa46"


TextTheorem: "Over ZF, the axiom of choice is equivalent to each of the
              following principles: every set can be well ordered; every
              partially ordered set in which each chain has an upper bound has a
              maximal element (Zorn's lemma); and every surjective function
              admits a right inverse. The equivalences allow choice to enter a
              proof in the form best adapted to its subject."
Id: "694788bb-a939-406b-8a3f-62730d8d0399"


SectionTitle: "Cardinality and Cantor's Theorem"
Id: "ce8978c6-895a-4528-bc27-a1796c5fc751"


[A \.equinumerous.with./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(bijective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ and $B?$ have the same cardinality"
. written: "|A?| = |B?|"
. description: "Two sets have the same cardinality when a bijection exists
                between them."
Id: "5ba55513-d2b3-458e-afd9-2fbd7507d47f"


[A \.cardinality.leq./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "cardinality of $A?$ is at most cardinality of $B?$"
. written: "|A?| \leq |B?|"
. description: "$|A|\leq|B|$ means that an injection from $A$ to $B$ exists."
Id: "d3cc912e-a944-4a5f-8cc8-cca18c511746"


[\(finite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. exists: n is \natural
  suchThat: X \.equinumerous.with./ \natural.initial.segment:before{n}
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
. description: "A set is finite when it is bijective with the natural numbers
                strictly below some natural number $n$."
Id: "88d1e87d-f649-4246-b417-7d3ca4c7f24c"


[\(countably.infinite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X \.equinumerous.with./ \naturals
Documented:
. adjective: "countably infinite"
. description: "A countably infinite set is one admitting a bijection with the
                natural numbers."
Id: "7337910a-2ecc-46f7-95a7-9cc5812af19e"


[\(countable)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. anyOf:
  . X is? \(finite)::set
  . X is? \(countably.infinite)::set
Documented:
. adjective: "countable"
. description: "A set is countable when it is finite or countably infinite."
Id: "9725d8a1-0c65-491e-901b-ee23b8e87888"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. A \.cardinality.leq./ B
. B \.cardinality.leq./ A
then: A \.equinumerous.with./ B
Documented:
. called: "Cantor--Schroeder--Bernstein Theorem"
. description: "Mutual injections between two sets imply the existence of a
                bijection."
Id: "5ad0453f-1203-4467-949b-4c8a8697b5b3"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then:
. not: A \.equinumerous.with./ \power.set:of{A}
Documented:
. called: "Cantor's Theorem"
. description: "No set is equinumerous with its power set."
Id: "83a30248-2172-45d8-934a-e7a54be66b12"


Text: "Cantor's diagonal argument illustrates the foundational discipline of the
       chapter. The set $D$ exists by separation from $A$, not by unrestricted
       comprehension. Its definition then converts the assumption that $f$ lists
       every subset of $A$ into the contradiction $d\in D\iff d\notin D$ at a
       purported index $d$ for $D$."
Id: "8d163eb8-a148-43d1-80b3-38c54e51e278"
